  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-114191",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{w}^{3}-4{w}^{2}+12w-16 & = \\\\ (3{w}^{3}-4{w}^{2})+(12w-16) & = \\\\ {w}^{2}(3w-4)+4(3w-4) & = \\\\ (3w-4)\\cdot {w}^{2}+(3w-4)\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ (3w-4)({w}^{2}+4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(3w-4)({w}^{2}+4)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(3w-4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w-4 & =0 \\\\ 3w & =4 \\\\ w & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({w}^{2}+4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {w}^{2} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince a squared real number cannot be negative, the equation ${w}^{2}+4=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore,  $w=\\frac{4}{3}$ is the only real solution.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
